  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Blue Economy and Inclusive Development for Climate Justice (BID4CJ)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The coast of Bangladesh is one of the countries most vulnerable areas to climate change. However, Bangladesh also views this area as at the forefront of its future development &#x2013; focusing on the opportunities of blue economy or harnessing the benefits of the ocean.  The countries masterplans like the Delta 2100 and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan 2041, are focused on an integrated and rapid development approach for the next 20 years. The country is urbanising its rural areas and also developing hundreds of economic zones and export processing zones, which will require additional supply of water and energy, as well as greater connectivity through multi -model transportation. As one of the leaders in climate negotiations on behalf of the world&apos;s most vulnerable countries, Bangladesh ambitiously aims to install 40 gigawatts (GW) by 2041, of which 40% would be from renewable sources. Has an ambitious plan to continue shifting its total energy supply to renewables, while meeting increased demand due to economic growth. This fast export -driven economic transition means that coastal communities vulnerable to climate change also have to cope with changes to the landscape, including dealing with potential environmental and social impacts as well as loss and damage from climate change. . Oxfam will work to ensure that pathways to sustainable development are people -centred, inclusive, environmentally responsible and climate -smart  Accordingly, BID4CJ will equip communities with the capacity, power, and evidence for influence to negotiate with government and the private sector so as to harness their fair share of sustainable economic growth in coastal areas;  will mobilise communities to manage and restore key coastal and marine ecosystems that provide livelihoods and protection from climatic vulnerabilities, and will promote green businesses (e.g., businesses that sustainably use low energy and natural resources as well as cut carbon emission) that directly contribute to community</narrative>
  </description>
